public class NameSorter {

    /**
     * Sorts an array of names in ascending (alphabetical) order
     * using a manual bubble sort implementation.
     * Does not use Arrays.sort(), Collections.sort(), or any built-in sorting method.
     *
     * @param names the array of names to sort
     */
    public static void sortNames(String[] names) {
        int n = names.length;
        boolean swapped;

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            swapped = false;
            for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
                if (names[j].compareTo(names[j + 1]) > 0) {
                    // Swap
                    String temp = names[j];
                    names[j] = names[j + 1];
                    names[j + 1] = temp;
                    swapped = true;
                }
            }
            // If no two elements were swapped, array is already sorted
            if (!swapped) {
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] names = {"Charlie", "Alice", "Eve", "Bob", "David"};

        sortNames(names);

        for (String name : names) {
            System.out.println(name);
        }
        // Output:
        // Alice
        // Bob
        // Charlie
        // David
        // Eve
    }
}
```

This implementation uses **bubble sort**, comparing names with `String.compareTo()` (which does lexicographic comparison) and swapping elements manually — no built-in sort methods are used.

If you'd like, I can provide a different algorithm instead (e.g., insertion sort, selection sort, or quicksort) or make it case-insensitive.

