import java.util.Arrays;

public class QuickSortNames {

    /**
     * Sorts an array of names in descending order using quicksort.
     * Comparison is case-insensitive.
     *
     * @param names the array of names to sort
     * @return a new array with names sorted in descending order (case-insensitive)
     */
    public static String[] sortNamesDescending(String[] names) {
        String[] result = Arrays.copyOf(names, names.length);
        quickSort(result, 0, result.length - 1);
        return result;
    }

    private static void quickSort(String[] arr, int low, int high) {
        if (low < high) {
            int pivotIndex = partition(arr, low, high);
            quickSort(arr, low, pivotIndex - 1);
            quickSort(arr, pivotIndex + 1, high);
        }
    }

    private static int partition(String[] arr, int low, int high) {
        String pivot = arr[high];
        int i = low - 1;

        for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
            // Descending order: elements greater than pivot (case-insensitive) go first
            if (arr[j].compareToIgnoreCase(pivot) > 0) {
                i++;
                swap(arr, i, j);
            }
        }
        swap(arr, i + 1, high);
        return i + 1;
    }

    private static void swap(String[] arr, int i, int j) {
        String temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = temp;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] names = {"charlie", "Alice", "eve", "Bob", "david"};

        String[] sorted = sortNamesDescending(names);

        for (String name : sorted) {
            System.out.println(name);
        }
        // Output (descending, case-insensitive):
        // eve
        // david
        // charlie
        // Bob
        // Alice
    }
}
```

**Notes:**
- Uses `compareToIgnoreCase()` for case-insensitive comparison.
- Implements quicksort with Lomuto partitioning, adapted for descending order by reversing the comparison direction.
- Returns a new array rather than mutating the input, preserving the original array.
- Average time complexity: O(n log n); worst case: O(n²) (e.g., already sorted input with poor pivot choice).

If you'd like, I can provide a randomized pivot version to avoid worst-case behavior on sorted inputs, or an in-place version that skips returning a new array.

